  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., one where a larger portion of the probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones). To evaluate this, we must determine the skewness of each lottery. Lottery A offers a 45% chance of 6000 Euros and 55% chance of 0 Euros. This means the favorable outcome (6000) has less than 50% probability, so the distribution is right-skewed (more mass on the lower outcome). Lottery B offers a 90% chance of 3000 Euros and 10% chance of 0 Euros. Here, the favorable outcome (3000) has a very high probability (90%), so the distribution is left-skewed (most mass on the higher of the two outcomes). Therefore, Lottery A is more right-skewed than Lottery B. The decision reason would prefer Lottery A. The relevant information (probabilities and outcomes) is available to assess skewness, so the reason can be applied.",
  "step_2": "The individual reports studying both options multiple times to compare them. They explicitly calculated the expected value (\"mittlere zu erwartende Gewinns\") and found both options to be equal. Then, they state that they chose Option 2 (Lottery B) due to risk minimization, which they habitually use in such cases. The information considered includes expected value and risk, with a focus on minimizing risk. The justification is based on a general heuristic of risk minimization, not on the shape of the distribution or the concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes. There is no mention of skewness, asymmetry, or probability weighting on bad outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, particularly favoring more right-skewed lotteries (where unfavorable outcomes have higher probability). The individual, however, considers expected value and then applies a risk-minimization heuristic. Risk minimization might align with preferring higher probability of a positive outcome, which would favor Lottery B (90% chance of 3000), but this is conceptually different from preferring greater probability on unfavorable outcomes (which defines right-skew). The individual’s reasoning moves in the opposite direction: they prefer avoiding risk, i.e., avoiding the chance of zero, which means avoiding unfavorable outcomes—this contradicts the definition of preferring right-skew. Moreover, the reason’s preference would be for Lottery A, but the individual chooses Lottery B, though choice alone is not decisive. Crucially, the cognitive elements of the reason (assessing where probability mass lies relative to favorable/unfavorable outcomes) are absent in the report. The individual does not consider the skewness-related information, nor do they use a rule that aligns with preferring more right-skewed distributions. Therefore, there is no evidence the reason was used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
